  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nAs continues to be highlighted, we are anticipating an active \npattern through midweek with passing disturbances in the broad upper \ntrough lending to lift in the vicinity of a nearly stationary \nboundary that will set up in the upper Ohio Valley. Several rounds \nof showers and storms are anticipated in the W-NW flow, with the \nfirst potentially arriving overnight into Monday morning.\n\nThe latest CAMS suggest the convective complex currently over Lake \nMichigan will continue to drop southwestward through the remainder \nof the day today, most likely diving just west of our region into \ncentral/western Ohio tonight. As this occurs, some model \nguidance suggests some storms will develop along a boundary over\nour region. Given the timing of the storms, generally think \nthese will remain sub-severe/elevated if storms develop at all. \nIf storms do fire, this could slow the generation of additional \nstorms until much later in the day on Monday. However, the \nalternative scenario is that this convective complex completely \ndies off and leaves an outflow for convective initiation earlier\nin the afternoon, which some other model guidance suggests. \nRegardless, there appears to be a period of time Monday where \nthere is sufficient shear and instability for strong/severe \nstorms. Damaging wind gusts remain the primary threat, but hail \nand tornado risks also are present. In addition, given the \ncontinued presence of above-normal precipitable water values \n(90% of climo), as well as the potential for some training \nstorms along the boundary, WPC continues to highlight our region\nin a Slight (level 2 of 4) Risk for excessive rain for Monday \ninto Monday night. \n\nSevere/heavy rain risks continue for Tuesday and Wednesday with \nfocus along the aforementioned wavering boundary. Machine-learning \nguidance is in support of this. There is some suggestion in a slight \nsouthwestward shift in the heaviest precipitation from the runs \nyesterday, which could spare some of the region from the axis of \nheaviest rainfall but this will need to be monitored. The stronger \nGreat Lakes shortwave may finally push the boundary definitively \nsouth of the region by Thursday/Friday, leading to less threatening \nweather.",
